Prepare atoms, electrons and chemical bonds (@mat-atomic-entry: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @materials-engineering, #source "../../../chemistry/general-chemistry/sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/Fundamentals-of-Materials-Science-MIT-3-012.lingua#r_1JE4A3P3KKM0FZXERWFATXCS1Z", #materials-engineering "Energy-force-equilibrium-and-bond-stiffness.lingua#r_3B2ZTB5HFEYVF17YBGNEMXE117") { r_0YK3J5464VDN2WDCRDEYCQ1CXN
**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../../../chemistry/general-chemistry/sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapters 2, 6–8, complete worked examples and substantial exercises; [Fundamentals of Materials Science — MIT 3.012](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Materials-Science-MIT-3-012.lingua#r_1JE4A3P3KKM0FZXERWFATXCS1Z), structure/bonding lectures 1–11 progressively. The chemical textbook supplies the slower introduction before the mathematical slides.

Begin with atoms, nuclei, electron count, ions and chemical composition. Then distinguish an electron's quantum state from a little orbit drawn around a nucleus. Energy levels, occupation and interactions support a model of bonding; a periodic-table position is a clue, not a complete prediction of a material.

Read the shared general-chemistry atomic and bonding Records when these ideas are unfamiliar. Practice electron accounting, simple molecular geometry and the relation between a model and an observation before moving to extended solids.

**Work:** Explain one observation that requires more than a collection of rigid neutral spheres. Compare localized-bond and delocalized-state descriptions, naming the quantities each can address. Derive a simple energy-level result from the supplied mathematical preparation and state why it does not yet describe an actual many-electron solid.

**Return:** [Materials engineering](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
**Continue in materials engineering:** [Energy, force, equilibrium and bond stiffness](Energy-force-equilibrium-and-bond-stiffness.lingua#r_3B2ZTB5HFEYVF17YBGNEMXE117).

